Multiple inheritance in C++ is a feature where one class can inherit from more than one classes. The inheriting class is known to be derived class which inherits from more than one base classes. That is how it is possible for a derived class to exhibit the characteristics of more than one base class.

Let's consider an example for multiple inheritance:

```
// Base class Shape
class Shape
{
   public:
      void setWidth(int w)
      {
         width = w;
      }
      void setHeight(int h)
      {
         height = h;
      }
   protected:
      int width;
      int height;
};

// Base class PaintCost
class PaintCost
{
   public:
      int getCost(int area)
      {
         return area * 70;
      }
};

// Derived class
class Rectangle: public Shape, public PaintCost
{
   public:
      int getArea()
      { 
         return (width * height); 
      }
};

int main(void)
{
   Rectangle Rect;
   int area;

   Rect.setWidth(5);
   Rect.setHeight(7);

   area = Rect.getArea();

   // Print the total cost of painting
   cout << "Total paint cost : " << Rect.getCost(area) << endl;

   return 0;
}
```

In this example, Rectangle is a derived class. Here, it inherits properties from both the Shape and PaintCost classes which are base classes. In the main function, we get the cost of painting a rectangle with an area of 35 square units.

It is important to note that multiple inheritance can lead to a lot of confusion when two base classes implement a method with the same name.

Also, it needs to be handled with care because it can lead to a lot of complications if not handled properly such as the infamous Diamond problem. C++ does provide ways to handle such problems through the concepts such as virtual base class.

In a switch statement, the "break" keyword is used to exit the switch block and prevent the execution of subsequent cases. Without using the "break" keyword, the switch statement will fall through to the next case, continuing the execution until a "break" or the end of the switch block is encountered.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
int day = 2;
String dayOfWeek;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        dayOfWeek = "Monday";
        break;
    case 2:
        dayOfWeek = "Tuesday";
        break;
    case 3:
        dayOfWeek = "Wednesday";
        break;
    case 4:
        dayOfWeek = "Thursday";
        break;
    case 5:
        dayOfWeek = "Friday";
        break;
    default:
        dayOfWeek = "Invalid day";
}

System.out.println(dayOfWeek);
```

In this example, if the value of `day` is 2, the output will be "Tuesday" because the corresponding case is executed, and then the "break" statement is encountered, exiting the switch block. If the "break" statements were not present, the switch statement would fall through to the next case, resulting in "TuesdayWednesdayThursdayFriday" being assigned to `dayOfWeek` and printed.

Using the "break" keyword is important to ensure that only the desired case is executed and to prevent unintended fall-through behavior. Without the "break" keyword, multiple cases may be executed, leading to incorrect or unexpected results.

